What potions do you think are worth diluting, and which ones are worth
saving?

I tend to dilute:

Booze (saving a couple for self-confusion in the early game)
Fruit juice
See invisible (usually get that as an intrinsic in the Mines)
Sickness
Confusion
Hallucination
Restore Ability (use unihorns for that)
Sleeping
Blindness
Invisibility (zap self with wand when I have the chance)
Monster Detection
Object Detection (rethinking this)
Levitation
Acid
Oil (unless I don't have a permanent light source)
Paralysis

I tend to keep:

Full Healing
Healing          /* Most of these for alchemizing  *
Extra Healing     * into !oFH. Sometimes keep !oGE *
Speed             * to build up mana.              */
Gain Energy
Gain Level       /* For levelling up to the Quest, *
                  * for alchemy, or for rising up  *
                  * a dungeon level (esp. out of   *
                  * the Sanctum.                   */
Enlightenment
Polymorph
Gain Ability     /* Mostly for getting In up for    *
                  * spellcasting. If I have a lot   *
                  * of !oFH, I sometimes alchemize  *
                  * some of them into !oGA for this *
                  * purpose.                        */

I obviously don't use "attack" potions (blindness, confusion,
sickness). Does anyone else? I should try using lit potions of
oil--more for the fun of making molotov cocktails in the dungeon than
anything else.

I'm rethinking diluting !oOD. I've seen some people suggesting their
usefulness in Gehennom, which makes sense. OTOH, I tend to get stacks
of these, and when I start diluting, I generally dilute stacks first
(because at that point I'm relying on fountains and don't know how
many dips I'll get). If I start saving them, it would make sense to
save 20-30 of them to see objects on every level of Gehennom. That's
probably as many as I get in an entire game, usually. Plus, I use up a
lot of holy water, and find it one of the most useful things in the
game. Cutting back on that many holy water potions might make the game
significantly more difficult; I don't know if what I'd find in
Gehennom would be worth it.
